You rarely need this table directly, but it is the shared foundation the estimators are built on,
and it is handy when you want to check counts by hand or drive a custom calculation.

## Next steps

You now know how to represent survival data with `Surv()` or `event_time()`, how to describe a
response by column names with a formula or `Outcome`, and how to handle its event coding with
expressions. From here you can bring in
your own data or start estimating.

- [Data sources and formats](data-sources.qmd) shows how to load data from pandas, Polars, and
other backends, how to compute durations from timestamps and encode event indicators from compound
status columns, and lists the datasets bundled with the package.
- [Estimating survival with Kaplan-Meier](kaplan-meier.qmd) shows how to estimate and summarize
the survival curve.
- [Comparing groups](comparing-groups.qmd) covers the log-rank family of tests.
- The [Quick start](quick-start.qmd) is a fast tour of everything if you prefer to skim first.
